1: # Multilayer Detection of AI-Generated Content
2: 
3: ## Executive conclusion
4: 
5: There is no single “foolproof” detector for AI-generated image, video, text, or audio. The most reliable approach is **layered verification**: establish provenance first, perform forensic analysis second, use modality-specific machine-learning detectors third, test cross-modal consistency, and finally verify the surrounding real-world claim.
6: 
7: A detector should therefore report **“evidence supporting synthetic/manipulated origin”**, not absolute certainty. Current research shows that detectors can perform very well on familiar benchmark data but often fail on unseen generators, compression, paraphrasing, editing, and adversarial attacks. For example, a recent cross-modal survey reports 10–15% performance degradation under out-of-distribution conditions and more than 80% attack success for some white-box adversarial attacks. [link.springer](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10462-026-11608-4?error=cookies_not_supported&code=d3d2a35a-dd8d-4deb-9de2-cd7358c6b6ad)
8: 
9: ***
10: 
11: ## 1. Threat model
12: 
13: Before detecting anything, define what you are looking for:
14: 
15: | Category | Meaning | Example |
16: |---|---|---|
17: | Fully synthetic | Entire content generated by AI | Text-to-image portrait |
18: | Partially synthetic | Real content altered by AI | Face swap or AI voice overdub |
19: | AI-assisted | Human content substantially edited by AI | Paraphrased essay or noise-cleaned speech |
20: | Replayed content | Real recording replayed or re-recorded | Voice played through a speaker |
21: | Miscontextualized | Genuine content presented with a false claim | Old video labelled as a current event |
22: | Hybrid | Several real and synthetic components | Real video, cloned voice, generated subtitles |
23: 
24: The detector must answer at least three separate questions:
25: 
26: 1. **Was the file altered?**
27: 2. **Was AI involved?**
28: 3. **Is the claim or context true?**
29: 
30: These questions are not equivalent. A real photograph can be misleading, and an AI-generated image can accurately illustrate an event.
31: 
32: ***
33: 
34: # 2. Evidence hierarchy
35: 
36: Use the following layers in order.
37: 
38: ## Layer A: Provenance and chain of custody
39: 
40: Provenance is usually stronger than visual guessing.
41: 
42: Collect:
43: 
44: - Original file rather than a screenshot or re-upload.
45: - Cryptographic hash, such as SHA-256.
46: - Download URL, account, timestamp, and surrounding post.
47: - Original EXIF/XMP/ICC metadata.
48: - Editing history, if available.
49: - Device, camera, recorder, or software information.
50: - Earlier versions found through reverse search.
51: - Content Credentials/C2PA manifest.
52: 
53: A C2PA Content Credential is a cryptographically signed, tamper-evident record that can describe an asset’s origin, edits, and AI-tool involvement. It is useful when present and valid, but its absence does **not** prove that the content is human-made because metadata can be removed during export, screenshotting, or platform processing. [c2pa](https://c2pa.org/a-new-implementation-guide-for-content-credentials/)
54: 
55: ### File hash
56: 
57: For a file \(x\), compute:
58: 
59: \[
60: h = \operatorname{SHA256}(x)
61: \]
62: 
63: If two files have different hashes, they are not byte-identical. However, a changed hash does not necessarily mean visual manipulation; even a harmless metadata change produces a new hash.
64: 
65: ### Provenance confidence
66: 
67: A practical provenance score can be defined as:
68: 
69: \[
70: P = w_c C + w_s S + w_m M + w_h H
71: \]
72: 
73: where:
74: 
75: - \(C\): valid C2PA/content-credential evidence.
76: - \(S\): trusted source and publication history.
77: - \(M\): metadata consistency.
78: - \(H\): hash and chain-of-custody integrity.
79: - \(w_i\): weights summing to 1.
80: 
81: Do not treat \(P\) as a probability unless calibrated on representative data. It is an evidence score.
82: 
83: ### Recommended provenance outcomes
84: 
85: - **Verified origin:** valid signature, trusted capture device, consistent history.
86: - **Verified AI origin:** signed record explicitly identifies a generative or editing operation.
87: - **Unverified:** no reliable provenance available.
88: - **Contradictory:** metadata, source history, and media content disagree.
89: 
90: ***
91: 
92: ## Layer B: Classical forensic analysis
93: 
94: This layer examines how the file was created, encoded, and edited.
95: 
96: ### General forensic features
97: 
98: Useful signals include:
99: 
100: - JPEG quantization tables.
101: - Double-compression traces.
102: - Resampling and interpolation artifacts.
103: - Camera sensor noise or Photo-Response Non-Uniformity.
104: - CFA/demosaicing patterns.
105: - Chromatic aberration.
106: - Noise consistency across regions.
107: - Localized blur or sharpening.
108: - Inconsistent color spaces.
109: - Copy-move and splicing boundaries.
110: - Missing or suspicious metadata.
111: - Frequency-domain abnormalities.
112: 
113: For an image \(I(x,y)\), a two-dimensional Fourier transform is:
114: 
115: \[
116: F(u,v)=\sum_x\sum_y I(x,y)e^{-j2\pi(ux+vy)}
117: \]
118: 
119: The power spectrum is:
120: 
121: \[
122: S(u,v)=|F(u,v)|^2
123: \]
124: 
125: Synthetic images may contain unusual periodic or high-frequency patterns. A detector may calculate a frequency statistic:
126: 
127: \[
128: f_{\text{freq}}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{(u,v)\in \Omega}
129: \left|S(u,v)-\mu_{\text{real}}(u,v)\right|
130: \]
131: 
132: where \(\Omega\) is a selected frequency region. This can help, but it is not universal: resizing, JPEG compression, sharpening, and new generation methods can change the signal.
133: 
134: ### Noise-residual analysis
135: 
136: Let \(I\) be an image and \(L(I)\) a denoised version:
137: 
138: \[
139: R = I - L(I)
140: \]
141: 
142: For a natural camera image, \(R\) may contain sensor noise and optical artifacts. For a generated image, noise may be spatially inconsistent or absent. A useful consistency measure is:
143: 
144: \[
145: V_R = \operatorname{Var}\left(R-\hat{R}_{\text{camera}}\right)
146: \]
147: 
148: Again, this is evidence, not proof. A real image that has been heavily denoised may resemble a synthetic image.
149: 
150: ***
151: 
152: # 3. Image detection
153: 
154: ## 3.1 Signals to inspect
155: 
156: ### Semantic and geometric inconsistencies
157: 
158: Check:
159: 
160: - Hands, fingers, teeth, ears, and jewelry.
161: - Text and logos.
162: - Reflections and shadows.
163: - Perspective and vanishing points.
164: - Repeated objects or textures.
165: - Impossible object intersections.
166: - Background geometry.
167: - Lighting direction.
168: - Facial symmetry.
169: - Fine hair and transparent objects.
170: 
171: These clues are useful for manual screening but are becoming less reliable as image generators improve.
172: 
173: ### Pixel-level forensic signals
174: 
175: Use:
176: 
177: - Local noise inconsistency.
178: - Frequency-spectrum features.
179: - JPEG and resampling analysis.
180: - Edge and texture statistics.
181: - Camera fingerprint mismatch.
182: - Patch-level anomaly detection.
183: 
184: ### Neural image detectors
185: 
186: A typical classifier receives an image or image patches and estimates:
187: 
188: \[
189: p(y=1\mid I)=\sigma(f_\theta(I))
190: \]
191: 
192: where \(y=1\) can represent synthetic content, \(f_\theta\) is a neural network, and \(\sigma\) is the sigmoid function.
193: 
194: Better systems use multiple crops:
195: 
196: \[
197: p_{\text{image}}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}p(y=1\mid I_k)
198: \]
199: 
200: This reduces the risk that a detector relies on one irrelevant region.
201: 
202: ### Important limitation
203: 
204: A 2026 zero-shot benchmark covering 16 detector families, 23 variants, 12 datasets, 2.6 million images, and 291 generators found no universal winner. Detector accuracy varied substantially, and some modern commercial generators achieved only 18–30% average detection accuracy in the tested setting. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.07814)
205: 
206: Therefore, never trust a single image detector, especially when:
207: 
208: - The image is small.
209: - It was screenshot or re-encoded.
210: - It has been cropped.
211: - It contains strong filters.
212: - It comes from an unknown generator.
213: - It is a hybrid human/AI composition.
214: 
215: ## 3.2 Image decision formula
216: 
217: Let:
218: 
219: - \(p_n\): neural detector score.
220: - \(p_f\): forensic score.
221: - \(p_p\): provenance score.
222: - \(p_r\): reverse-search/source score.
223: - \(q\): quality and integrity factor.
224: 
225: A calibrated fusion model may be:
226: 
227: \[
228: z = b + w_n\operatorname{logit}(p_n)
229: + w_f\operatorname{logit}(p_f)
230: + w_p\operatorname{logit}(p_p)
231: + w_r\operatorname{logit}(p_r)
232: + w_q q
233: \]
234: 
235: \[
236: p_{\text{synthetic}}=\sigma(z)
237: \]
238: 
239: In practice, train or calibrate this fusion model using data from **unseen generators and realistic post-processing**, not only random train/test splits.
240: 
241: ***
242: 
243: # 4. Video detection
244: 
245: Video requires both image-level and time-level analysis.
246: 
247: ## 4.1 Frame-level analysis
248: 
249: Sample frames at regular intervals and around suspicious transitions. For each frame, inspect:
250: 
251: - Face boundaries.
252: - Hair and ear regions.
253: - Teeth and mouth interiors.
254: - Skin texture.
255: - Reflections.
256: - Shadows.
257: - Background continuity.
258: - Hands and object interaction.
259: - Compression differences between regions.
260: 
261: A frame detector produces:
262: 
263: \[
264: p_t = P(\text{fake}\mid I_t)
265: \]
266: 
267: Do not simply use the maximum frame score because one corrupted frame may create a false alarm. Use robust aggregation:
268: 
269: \[
270: p_{\text{frame}} =
271: \operatorname{median}(p_1,\ldots,p_T)
272: +\lambda Q_{0.9}(p_1,\ldots,p_T)
273: \]
274: 
275: where \(Q_{0.9}\) is the 90th percentile.
276: 
277: ## 4.2 Temporal consistency
278: 
279: For a tracked facial landmark \(l_t\), calculate velocity and acceleration:
280: 
281: \[
282: v_t=l_t-l_{t-1}
283: \]
284: 
285: \[
286: a_t=v_t-v_{t-1}
287: \]
288: 
289: Abrupt, repeated or localized changes in \(v_t\) and \(a_t\) can indicate synthesis or face replacement.
290: 
291: For optical flow \(F_t(x,y)\), compare neighboring motion fields:
292: 
293: \[
294: E_{\text{flow}}=
295: \frac{1}{T-1}\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}
296: \left\|F_{t+1}-F_t\right\|_1
297: \]
298: 
299: High error is not automatically fake: camera cuts, fast motion, occlusion, low frame rate, and compression can also cause it.
300: 
301: Useful temporal signals include:
302: 
303: - Flickering skin or hair.
304: - Inconsistent head pose.
305: - Changing identity embeddings.
306: - Unnatural blinking or gaze.
307: - Mouth-shape discontinuity.
308: - Frame-to-frame texture instability.
309: - Object boundaries that “swim.”
310: - Inconsistent shadows or reflections.
311: 
312: Research commonly categorizes video detection into spatial, temporal, frequency, and data-driven methods. Frame-by-frame generation can produce flicker and discontinuity, while modern methods increasingly model spatiotemporal features. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/html/2403.17881v5)
313: 
314: ## 4.3 Audio–visual consistency
315: 
316: For talking-head videos, compare the audio phoneme sequence with visible mouth movement.
317: 
318: Let \(a_t\) be an audio embedding and \(v_t\) a visual mouth embedding. A simple synchronization score is:
319: 
320: \[
321: S_{\text{sync}}=
322: \max_{\delta\in[-\Delta,\Delta]}
323: \operatorname{cos}(a_t,v_{t+\delta})
324: \]
325: 
326: A persistent abnormal delay \(\delta\), weak maximum similarity, or inconsistent timing can indicate dubbing or lip-sync manipulation.
327: 
328: The analysis should also check:
329: 
330: - Whether mouth shapes correspond to phonemes.
331: - Whether jaw motion matches speech energy.
332: - Whether emotional expression matches vocal prosody.
333: - Whether the voice and face have consistent identity.
334: - Whether the audio has been independently re-recorded.
335: 
336: Ordinary dubbing, network delay, subtitles, and editing can also create misalignment, so synchronization must be interpreted with the editing history.
337: 
338: ***
339: 
340: # 5. Audio detection
341: 
342: ## 5.1 Signal-level features
343: 
344: Extract features from short overlapping windows.
345: 
346: ### Short-Time Fourier Transform
347: 
348: \[
349: X(t,f)=\sum_n x[n]w[n-t]e^{-j2\pi fn}
350: \]
351: 
352: From the spectrogram, inspect:
353: 
354: - Harmonic continuity.
355: - Formant transitions.
356: - Phase irregularities.
357: - High-frequency roll-off.
358: - Spectral flatness.
359: - Background-noise consistency.
360: - Breath and micro-prosody.
361: - Pitch transitions.
362: - Unnatural pauses.
363: - Vocoder or codec artifacts.
364: 
365: ### Spectral features
366: 
367: Spectral centroid:
368: 
369: \[
370: C_t=\frac{\sum_f f|X(t,f)|}{\sum_f |X(t,f)|}
371: \]
372: 
373: Spectral flatness:
374: 
375: \[
376: F_t=
377: \frac{\exp\left(\frac{1}{N}\sum_f \ln P_t(f)\right)}
378: {\frac{1}{N}\sum_f P_t(f)}
379: \]
380: 
381: Zero-crossing rate:
382: 
383: \[
384: Z_t=\frac{1}{2(N-1)}
385: \sum_{n=1}^{N-1}
386: |\operatorname{sgn}(x[n])-\operatorname{sgn}(x[n-1])|
387: \]
388: 
389: Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients are also frequently used, although modern systems often combine them with learned speech representations.
390: 
391: ## 5.2 Voice-specific signals
392: 
393: Analyze:
394: 
395: - Pitch \(F_0\) continuity.
396: - Jitter and shimmer.
397: - Breath placement.
398: - Coarticulation.
399: - Consonant transitions.
400: - Emotion/prosody consistency.
401: - Room impulse response.
402: - Microphone noise.
403: - Speaker identity embeddings.
404: - Synchronization with background noise.
405: 
406: A synthesized voice may sound natural globally but contain small local abnormalities in phase, consonants, breaths, or prosody.
407: 
408: ## 5.3 Neural audio detectors
409: 
410: A common architecture is:
411: 
412: 1. Convert waveform to log-mel spectrogram or learned representation.
413: 2. Encode with a CNN, conformer, or pretrained speech model.
414: 3. Pool segment embeddings.
415: 4. Produce a calibrated spoof score.
416: 
417: \[
418: e_i = g_\theta(x_i), \qquad
419: p_i=\sigma(w^\top e_i+b)
420: \]
421: 
422: Aggregate across segments using quality-weighted averaging:
423: 
424: \[
425: p_{\text{audio}}=
426: \frac{\sum_i q_i p_i}{\sum_i q_i}
427: \]
428: 
429: where \(q_i\) reflects signal quality.
430: 
431: Evaluate using:
432: 
433: - Equal Error Rate (EER).
434: - ROC-AUC.
435: - False Acceptance Rate (FAR).
436: - False Rejection Rate (FRR).
437: - Tandem Detection Cost Function (t-DCF).
438: - Detection Error Tradeoff (DET) curves.
439: 
440: Audio results often collapse under noise, reverberation, MP3 compression, and unseen synthesis systems. Research specifically emphasizes realistic cross-dataset and noisy-condition testing rather than clean laboratory performance. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.13744)
441: 
442: ***
443: 
444: # 6. Text detection
445: 
446: Text is the most difficult modality for authorship attribution because text has no universal physical “AI fingerprint,” and human writing varies enormously.
447: 
448: ## 6.1 Passive detection
449: 
450: Passive detectors analyze:
451: 
452: - Token likelihood.
453: - Perplexity.
454: - Burstiness.
455: - Sentence-length variation.
456: - Repeated syntactic patterns.
457: - Function-word frequencies.
458: - Part-of-speech sequences.
459: - Vocabulary richness.
460: - Punctuation habits.
461: - Semantic repetition.
462: - Paragraph structure.
463: - Stylistic consistency across documents.
464: 
465: For a language model with token probabilities \(p(x_i\mid x_{<i})\), perplexity is:
466: 
467: \[
468: \operatorname{PPL}(x)=
469: \exp\left(
470: -\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}
471: \log p(x_i\mid x_{<i})
472: \right)
473: \]
474: 
475: Burstiness can be approximated by:
476: 
477: \[
478: B=\frac{\operatorname{Var}(L)}
479: {\operatorname{E}[L]+\epsilon}
480: \]
481: 
482: where \(L\) is sentence length. Low perplexity or regular sentence structure may be evidence, but neither proves AI authorship. Modern language models can produce varied text, and humans can write formulaically.
483: 
484: ## 6.2 Classifier-based detection
485: 
486: A text detector may use:
487: 
488: \[
489: p_{\text{AI}}=\sigma(f_\theta(x))
490: \]
491: 
492: with a transformer encoder, stylometric features, or both.
493: 
494: A more robust hybrid model is:
495: 
496: \[
497: z =
498: w_1z_{\text{transformer}}
499: +w_2z_{\text{stylometry}}
500: +w_3z_{\text{perplexity}}
501: +w_4z_{\text{retrieval}}
502: +b
503: \]
504: 
505: Then calibrate \(p=\sigma(z)\) on target-domain data.
506: 
507: ## 6.3 Watermarking
508: 
509: If the generating system inserted a watermark, detection may examine whether selected token classes occur more frequently than expected.
510: 
511: Let \(G\) be the set of green tokens and \(n_G\) the number of generated tokens in \(G\). If the expected fraction is \(\gamma\), a z-score is:
512: 
513: \[
514: z=
515: \frac{n_G-\gamma n}
516: {\sqrt{n\gamma(1-\gamma)}}
517: \]
518: 
519: A large \(z\) may indicate a watermark. However:
520: 
521: - The generator must support watermarking.
522: - The detector must know the key or algorithm.
523: - Paraphrasing, translation, editing, and short text weaken detection.
524: - Human text can randomly produce a high score.
525: 
526: ## 6.4 Retrieval and authorship evidence
527: 
528: For high-stakes cases, process evidence is stronger than a detector score:
529: 
530: - Draft history.
531: - Version-control commits.
532: - Notes and outlines.
533: - Browser or editor history.
534: - Oral explanation of the work.
535: - Consistency with prior writing.
536: - Citations and source trail.
537: - Document metadata.
538: - Institution-approved writing logs.
539: 
540: Current surveys classify text detection into passive intrinsic methods and active methods such as watermarking and semantic retrieval. They also identify paraphrasing and adversarial editing as major weaknesses. [sciopen](https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.32604/cmc.2025.073347)
541: 
542: A recent benchmark further reported that high in-domain scores do not imply cross-domain reliability; detectors may learn dataset-specific stylistic artifacts instead of stable machine-authorship signals. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23146)
543: 
544: ### Critical rule
545: 
546: Do not accuse someone of misconduct solely because an AI-text detector gives a high score. Detector outputs should be treated as screening evidence, not authorship proof. [jmris](http://jmris.com/index.php/jmris/article/view/23)
547: 
548: ***
549: 
550: # 7. Multimodal fusion
551: 
552: When content contains multiple modalities, combine independent evidence.
553: 
554: Let:
555: 
556: - \(z_I\): image score.
557: - \(z_V\): video-temporal score.
558: - \(z_A\): audio score.
559: - \(z_T\): transcript score.
560: - \(z_S\): source/provenance score.
561: - \(z_C\): cross-modal consistency score.
562: 
563: A basic fusion model is:
564: 
565: \[
566: z =
567: b+\sum_m w_m z_m
568: +\sum_{i<j}w_{ij}z_i z_j
569: \]
570: 
571: \[
572: p_{\text{fake}}=\sigma(z)
573: \]
574: 
575: A better model uses **quality-aware gating**:
576: 
577: \[
578: \alpha_m =
579: \frac{\exp(g_m(q_m))}
580: {\sum_j\exp(g_j(q_j))}
581: \]
582: 
583: \[
584: z=\sum_m \alpha_m z_m
585: \]
586: 
587: Here \(q_m\) measures modality reliability. For example:
588: 
589: - Downsampled video receives lower visual weight.
590: - Noisy audio receives lower audio weight.
591: - A very short transcript receives low text weight.
592: - A valid C2PA signature receives high provenance weight.
593: 
594: Do not average modalities blindly. If video is heavily compressed but audio is clean, the system should reduce the visual contribution.
595: 
596: ## Cross-modal checks
597: 
598: Evaluate:
599: 
600: - Lip movement versus phonemes.
601: - Facial emotion versus vocal prosody.
602: - Speaker identity versus face identity.
603: - Scene acoustics versus visible environment.
604: - Shadows and reflections versus claimed lighting.
605: - Transcript versus visible signs.
606: - Time, location, and weather versus the claimed event.
607: - Object movement versus physical constraints.
608: 
609: A contradiction across modalities is often more informative than an isolated artifact.
610: 
611: ***
612: 
613: # 8. A practical “defense-in-depth” pipeline
614: 
615: ## Stage 1: Preserve the evidence
616: 
617: 1. Download the original file.
618: 2. Store it read-only.
619: 3. Compute SHA-256.
620: 4. Record URL, account, date, and time.
621: 5. Preserve the post, caption, comments, and surrounding context.
622: 6. Avoid repeatedly opening and re-exporting the original.
623: 
624: ## Stage 2: Establish provenance
625: 
626: 1. Parse EXIF/XMP metadata.
627: 2. Validate C2PA credentials.
628: 3. Check whether the claimed camera, software, and timestamps are plausible.
629: 4. Search for earlier versions.
630: 5. Compare hashes and visual differences.
631: 6. Identify edits and re-encodings.
632: 
633: ## Stage 3: Run modality-specific tests
634: 
635: - Image: patch classifier, frequency features, noise residual, geometry.
636: - Video: frame classifier, temporal model, landmark dynamics, audio–visual synchronization.
637: - Audio: spectrogram classifier, speaker embedding, phase/prosody, channel consistency.
638: - Text: stylometry, calibrated classifier, watermark test if applicable, authorship history.
639: 
640: ## Stage 4: Stress-test the result
641: 
642: Run the same content through:
643: 
644: - JPEG or video compression.
645: - Resizing.
646: - Cropping.
647: - Screenshot conversion.
648: - Background noise.
649: - MP3/AAC transcoding.
650: - Paraphrasing.
651: - Translation.
652: - Re-recording.
653: - Different detector models.
654: 
655: If a decision changes easily, confidence must be reduced.
656: 
657: ## Stage 5: Cross-generator testing
658: 
659: A model trained on one generator may learn its specific fingerprints. Test against:
660: 
661: - Known generators.
662: - Unseen generators.
663: - Different resolutions.
664: - Different prompts and subjects.
665: - Human-edited synthetic content.
666: - Real content with similar compression.
667: 
668: Use generator-disjoint splits:
669: 
670: \[
671: \mathcal{G}_{\text{train}}
672: \cap
673: \mathcal{G}_{\text{test}}
674: =\varnothing
675: \]
676: 
677: Also use subject-disjoint and source-disjoint splits to prevent leakage.
678: 
679: ## Stage 6: Calibrate and report
680: 
681: A raw classifier score is not automatically a probability. Use calibration:
682: 
683: \[
684: p_{\text{cal}}=\operatorname{Calibrate}(s)
685: \]
686: 
687: Possible methods include:
688: 
689: - Platt scaling.
690: - Isotonic regression.
691: - Temperature scaling.
692: - Conformal prediction.
693: 
694: Report:
695: 
696: - Prediction.
697: - Confidence interval or prediction set.
698: - Evidence categories.
699: - Quality limitations.
700: - Detector versions.
701: - Threshold used.
702: - False-positive and false-negative rates.
703: - Whether the test was in-domain or out-of-domain.
704: - Exact regions or timestamps that caused suspicion.
705: 
706: ***
707: 
708: # 9. Decision thresholds
709: 
710: For high-stakes use, select thresholds according to the cost of errors.
711: 
712: Let:
713: 
714: - \(C_{FP}\): cost of falsely accusing real content.
715: - \(C_{FN}\): cost of missing synthetic content.
716: - \(P(F)\): prior probability of fake content.
717: - \(p\): calibrated posterior probability.
718: 
719: Choose “fake” when:
720: 
721: \[
722: C_{FP}(1-p)
723: <
724: C_{FN}p
725: \]
726: 
727: Therefore:
728: 
729: \[
730: p >
731: \frac{C_{FP}}
732: {C_{FP}+C_{FN}}
733: \]
734: 
735: If false accusations are extremely harmful, the threshold should be high and the system should abstain frequently.
736: 
737: Use three outcomes instead of binary classification:
738: 
739: - **Likely authentic**
740: - **Indeterminate**
741: - **Likely synthetic/manipulated**
742: 
743: The indeterminate region is essential. A system that always chooses real or fake will appear decisive but will be unsafe.
744: 
745: ***
746: 
747: # 10. Evaluation formulas
748: 
749: Accuracy alone is inadequate.
750: 
751: ## Confusion matrix
752: 
753: - True positive: fake correctly detected.
754: - False positive: real content incorrectly flagged.
755: - True negative: real content correctly accepted.
756: - False negative: fake content missed.
757: 
758: \[
759: \operatorname{Precision}=
760: \frac{TP}{TP+FP}
761: \]
762: 
763: \[
764: \operatorname{Recall}=
765: \frac{TP}{TP+FN}
766: \]
767: 
768: \[
769: F_1=
770: 2\cdot
771: \frac{\operatorname{Precision}\cdot\operatorname{Recall}}
772: {\operatorname{Precision}+\operatorname{Recall}}
773: \]
774: 
775: \[
776: \operatorname{FPR}=
777: \frac{FP}{FP+TN}
778: \]
779: 
780: \[
781: \operatorname{FNR}=
782: \frac{FN}{FN+TP}
783: \]
784: 
785: For forensic deployment, always publish FPR at the operating threshold. A 98% accuracy figure can still be unacceptable when the real-world prevalence of fake content is low.
786: 
787: ### Base-rate example
788: 
789: Suppose:
790: 
791: - Fake prevalence: \(1\%\).
792: - True-positive rate: \(95\%\).
793: - False-positive rate: \(2\%\).
794: 
795: Among 10,000 items:
796: 
797: - 100 are fake; 95 are detected.
798: - 9,900 are real; 198 are falsely flagged.
799: 
800: Thus:
801: 
802: \[
803: PPV=\frac{95}{95+198}\approx32.4\%
804: \]
805: 
806: Even with apparently strong detector performance, most flagged items would be false alarms in this scenario.
807: 
808: ***
809: 
810: # 11. Dataset and experiment design
811: 
812: A serious detector should be trained and tested with:
813: 
814: - Real images from different cameras and countries.
815: - GAN, diffusion, autoregressive, and neural-rendering outputs.
816: - Multiple generators and versions.
817: - Human edits of generated content.
818: - Different resolutions and codecs.
819: - Screenshots and social-media recompression.
820: - Different languages and writing abilities for text.
821: - Different microphones, rooms, speakers, and noise levels for audio.
822: - Face, age, gender, skin-tone, and lighting diversity.
823: - Content from sources not represented during training.
824: 
825: Use these splits:
826: 
827: 1. **Random split:** checks basic learning but is weak.
828: 2. **Generator-disjoint split:** tests unseen generators.
829: 3. **Source-disjoint split:** tests unseen creators/platforms.
830: 4. **Time-disjoint split:** tests model drift.
831: 5. **Cross-language split:** important for text.
832: 6. **Cross-codec split:** important for audio/video.
833: 7. **Adversarial split:** tests evasion.
834: 
835: A report should include bootstrap confidence intervals:
836: 
837: \[
838: CI_{95\%} =
839: [\hat{\theta}_{2.5\%},\hat{\theta}_{97.5\%}]
840: \]
841: 
842: where \(\hat{\theta}\) is recalculated across many bootstrap samples.
843: 
844: ***
845: 
846: # 12. Common failure modes
847: 
848: ## Detectors learn shortcuts
849: 
850: A model may detect:
851: 
852: - Resolution differences.
853: - Dataset-specific compression.
854: - Watermarks or logos.
855: - Generator-specific borders.
856: - Different captioning styles.
857: - Filename or metadata patterns.
858: 
859: This creates impressive benchmark scores but poor real-world generalization.
860: 
861: ## Post-processing removes evidence
862: 
863: Evidence can be weakened by:
864: 
865: - Screenshots.
866: - Cropping.
867: - Resizing.
868: - Blur.
869: - Sharpening.
870: - Re-encoding.
871: - Noise addition.
872: - Re-recording a screen or speaker.
873: - Paraphrasing text.
874: - Human editing of generated material.
875: 
876: ## Human–AI hybrids are ambiguous
877: 
878: A document may contain:
879: 
880: - Human ideas and AI polishing.
881: - AI draft with human rewriting.
882: - Human text translated by AI.
883: - Human audio with AI noise reduction.
884: - Real photograph with AI background replacement.
885: 
886: Binary labels are often scientifically inappropriate for these cases.
887: 
888: ## Bias and fairness
889: 
890: Text detectors can disproportionately flag non-native English writing and highly formal writing. Short text, multilingual text, student writing, and genre-specific language deserve separate validation. [jmris](http://jmris.com/index.php/jmris/article/view/23)
891: 
892: ## Adversarial adaptation
893: 
894: Once attackers understand the detector, they can optimize against it. A detector should be retrained and evaluated continuously, but no passive detector can guarantee permanent immunity.
895: 
896: ***
897: 
898: # 13. Recommended system architecture
899: 
900: For a production-grade research project, use this design:
901: 
902: ```text
903: Input
904:   |
905:   +-- Evidence preservation and hashing
906:   |
907:   +-- Provenance/C2PA verification
908:   |
909:   +-- File and codec analysis
910:   |
911:   +-- Modality quality assessment
912:   |
913:   +-- Image detector
914:   +-- Video temporal detector
915:   +-- Audio spoof detector
916:   +-- Text detector
917:   |
918:   +-- Cross-modal consistency analysis
919:   |
920:   +-- Source and reverse-search verification
921:   |
922:   +-- Calibrated fusion model
923:   |
924:   +-- Abstention and human review
925:   |
926:   +-- Explainable forensic report
927: ```
928: 
929: A suitable report schema is:
930: 
931: ```json
932: {
933:   "file_hash_sha256": "...",
934:   "provenance": {
935:     "c2pa_status": "valid|invalid|missing",
936:     "source_consistency": 0.0,
937:     "metadata_consistency": 0.0
938:   },
939:   "quality": {
940:     "resolution": "...",
941:     "compression": "...",
942:     "audio_snr_db": null
943:   },
944:   "modality_scores": {
945:     "image": 0.0,
946:     "video_temporal": 0.0,
947:     "audio": 0.0,
948:     "text": 0.0
949:   },
950:   "cross_modal_score": 0.0,
951:   "final_result": "likely_authentic|indeterminate|likely_synthetic",
952:   "limitations": [],
953:   "evidence_timestamps": []
954: }
955: ```
956: 
957: The model should also support **localization**:
958: 
959: - Image: suspicious bounding boxes or patches.
960: - Video: suspicious frames and time ranges.
961: - Audio: suspicious seconds or phoneme regions.
962: - Text: suspicious spans, but never treat them as definitive proof.
963: 
964: ***
965: 
966: # 14. What “foolproof” should mean
967: 
968: A realistic goal is not perfect classification. Define “foolproof” operationally:
969: 
970: - The system never makes a final accusation automatically.
971: - Every prediction has an evidence trail.
972: - It can abstain.
973: - It is tested on unseen generators.
974: - It reports false-positive rates.
975: - It preserves original evidence.
976: - It separates AI involvement from factual truth.
977: - It combines provenance, forensic, model, and contextual evidence.
978: - High-impact decisions require human review.
979: - The system is continuously re-evaluated against new generators and attacks.
980: 
981: The strongest result is usually:
982: 
983: > “The file has valid provenance showing creation by a named device/tool,”
984: 
985: or:
986: 
987: > “The content is likely manipulated because independent provenance, forensic, temporal, and audio–visual evidence agree.”
988: 
989: The weakest result is:
990: 
991: > “One detector gave 87%, therefore it is fake.”
992: 
993: ***
994: 
995: ## Final recommendations
996: 
997: For your AI/ML project, build a **multimodal evidence-fusion system**, not a single classifier:
998: 
999: 1. Start with SHA-256, metadata, C2PA, and source history.
1000: 2. Add separate image, video, audio, and text models.
1001: 3. Train with generator-disjoint and post-processing-heavy data.
1002: 4. Add audio–visual synchronization for video.
1003: 5. Calibrate scores and include an indeterminate class.
1004: 6. Evaluate FPR, FNR, EER, ROC-AUC, calibration, and cross-domain degradation.
1005: 7. Produce localized explanations and preserve the original evidence.
1006: 8. Never use text-detector scores alone for academic or employment decisions.
1007: 9. Treat provenance as stronger evidence than missing metadata.
1008: 10. Re-test the entire system whenever new generators or editing tools appear.
1009: 
1010: The current research consensus is clear: **robust authenticity verification is a layered forensic and provenance problem, not merely an AI classification problem**.

(End of file - total 1010 lines)
</content>
