<content>
1: **No detection method is fully foolproof.** Generative models (LLMs, diffusion models, TTS/voice conversion, video generators) improve rapidly, and detectors often drop near chance performance on the newest outputs (e.g., state-of-the-art video detectors fell from ~94% AUROC on older benchmarks like Celeb-DF++ to ~48–70% on 2026-era commercial/open models such as Grok, Kling, Veo, Wan). Human detection is also near chance (~52–62% accuracy across modalities in meta-analyses). Robust practice therefore requires a **multi-layer defense** combining passive forensic analysis, active provenance (watermarking/fingerprinting), ensembles, continuous retraining, and human-in-the-loop verification.
2: 
3: The following synthesizes major surveys and papers (including “A Practical Synthesis of Detecting AI-Generated Textual, Visual, and Audio Content” [2025], “Detecting Multimedia Generated by Large AI Models” [arXiv:2402.00045], deepfake reviews 2021–2026, DetectGPT-style work, watermarking literature, and audio surveys such as arXiv:2308.14970). Methods are grouped by modality and layer.
4: 
5: ### 1. Text Detection
6: **Layers and techniques:**
7: 
8: - **Statistical / zero-shot (model-likelihood):**  
9:   DetectGPT and variants analyze local curvature of the log-probability surface: AI text tends to lie in regions of negative curvature under the generating (or similar) model.  
10:   Core idea: score a passage \(x\) by comparing its log-probability to that of perturbed versions; large drops indicate machine origin.  
11:   Related: perplexity, entropy, log-rank, n-gram divergence, DNA-GPT-style probability divergence.  
12:   Strengths: no training data needed for a specific detector. Weaknesses: requires access to (or a proxy for) the source model; fails under heavy paraphrasing or mixed human–AI editing.
13: 
14: - **Stylometric / linguistic profiling:** Burstiness, vocabulary richness, syntactic patterns, psycholinguistic features, or “linguistic footprints.” Tree-based or RoBERTa classifiers trained on these.
15: 
16: - **Supervised classifiers:** Fine-tuned transformers (RoBERTa, etc.) on large real vs. generated corpora (HC3, MAGE, OpenLLMText, etc.). Strong in-domain; poor cross-model/domain generalization.
17: 
18: - **Watermarking (proactive, strongest when available):**  
19:   Green-list / red-list (Kirchenbauer et al.): at each token, hash the previous tokens + secret key to partition the vocabulary; bias sampling toward the “green” list. Detection uses a one-proportion z-test on the observed green-token fraction.  
20:   SynthID-Text (Google DeepMind, used in Gemini and variants by Anthropic): tournament sampling with a keyed scoring function; statistical excess of preferred tokens yields a z-score / p-value.  
21:   Detection does not require the original model, only the key. Robust to moderate editing; vulnerable to aggressive paraphrasing or rewriting by another LLM. Invisible Unicode watermarks exist but are trivial to strip.
22: 
23: - **Ensemble / hybrid:** Combine curvature, stylometry, classifier scores, and watermark checks. Bayesian multi-hypothesis frameworks handle unknown source models.
24: 
25: **Practical limits:** Short texts are hard; heavy post-editing or multi-LLM pipelines destroy signals. Human detectors remain near chance without training/feedback.
26: 
27: ### 2. Image Detection
28: **Layers and techniques:**
29: 
30: - **Forensic / artifact analysis (spatial):** CNN or transformer classifiers (Xception, EfficientNet, ResNet, ViT, CLIP-based) trained to spot blending boundaries, texture inconsistencies, unnatural lighting, anatomical errors (hands, teeth, eyes), or frequency-domain fingerprints left by GANs/diffusion models.  
31:   Frequency-domain methods exploit up-sampling artifacts or generator-specific spectral signatures.
32: 
33: - **Reconstruction / inversion-based (e.g., DIRE):** Measure how easily an image can be reconstructed by a diffusion model; synthetic images often reconstruct more faithfully.
34: 
35: - **Fingerprint / model attribution:** Train to identify the specific generator family or checkpoint (possible with large multi-generator datasets such as GenImage, ForenSynths, DFLIP-3K). Linguistic profiling of prompts paired with images is an emerging direction.
36: 
37: - **Watermarking / content credentials:** Invisible or robust watermarks embedded at generation time (e.g., SynthID-style for images); C2PA / Content Credentials for provenance metadata. Detection is reliable when the watermark is present and not stripped/attacked.
38: 
39: - **CLIP / foundation-model features + linear heads or contrastive learning:** Strong zero-shot or few-shot generalization across architectures.
40: 
41: **Practical limits:** Compression, resizing, screenshots, and adversarial perturbations destroy many spatial/frequency cues. Newest diffusion models leave weaker fingerprints; cross-generator generalization remains the main failure mode. Ensembles of spatial + frequency + CLIP features improve robustness.
42: 
43: ### 3. Video Detection
44: **Layers and techniques:**
45: 
46: - **Spatial (per-frame):** Same image methods applied frame-wise, then aggregated.
47: 
48: - **Temporal / physiological:** Optical flow, inter-frame consistency, lip-sync, blinking, pulse (rPPG from subtle skin color changes), head-pose dynamics, or biological signal absence. 3D CNNs, transformers (TimeSformer, VideoMAE), dual-branch (appearance + flow), or graph-based models.
49: 
50: - **Multimodal (audio-visual):** Lip-sync inconsistency, cross-modal attention fusion. Stronger than unimodal when both streams are available.
51: 
52: - **Watermarking / provenance:** Frame-level or temporal watermarks; content credentials.
53: 
54: **Practical limits:** Recent commercial text-to-video and image-to-video models (2025–2026) cause catastrophic drops in detector AUROC (often to near-chance). Temporal methods help but still fail on high-quality closed-source output. Compression and platform re-encoding further degrade performance.
55: 
56: ### 4. Audio Detection
57: **Layers and techniques:**
58: 
59: - **Spectral / handcrafted features:** MFCC, CQTspectrogram, log-spectrogram, jitter, shimmer, formant analysis fed to CNNs, SVMs, or ResNeXt-style back-ends.
60: 
61: - **Learnable / end-to-end:** Raw waveform or mel-spectrogram into CNNs, transformers, Wav2Vec2 / HuBERT / XLS-R foundation models (large multilingual pretraining yields better out-of-distribution performance).
62: 
63: - **Consistency / multi-view:** Acoustic vs. semantic representation consistency (e.g., LHCC-style low- vs. high-level feature fusion).
64: 
65: - **Watermarking:** Imperceptible audio watermarks or speaker-verification residuals.
66: 
67: **Practical limits:** High-quality neural TTS and voice conversion produce clean spectrograms; in-the-wild and cross-attack generalization is weak. Equal-error-rate (EER) on older ASVspoof benchmarks can be low, but real-world performance degrades sharply.
68: 
69: ### 5. Multimodal & Ensemble Strategies (Recommended Multi-Layer Architecture)
70: 1. **Provenance first:** Check for watermarks, C2PA credentials, or cryptographic signatures. If present and valid → high-confidence AI origin.
71: 2. **Passive forensic stack:**  
72:    - Modality-specific detectors (statistical + classifier + artifact).  
73:    - Cross-modal consistency (audio-visual lip-sync, text-image alignment, etc.).
74: 3. **Model attribution / open-set detection:** Identify generator family when possible; flag unknowns.
75: 4. **Ensemble scoring:** Weighted combination or meta-classifier of all signals; output calibrated confidence + uncertainty.
76: 5. **Robustness layers:** Adversarial training, domain adaptation, continual learning on new generators, compression-augmented training.
77: 6. **Human-in-the-loop:** Expert review for high-stakes decisions; feedback training improves human performance above chance.
78: 7. **Monitoring & adaptation:** Continuously evaluate against newest generators; retrain or fine-tune.
79: 
80: **Evaluation metrics commonly used:** Accuracy, AUC/AUROC, EER (audio), precision/recall/F1, cross-dataset generalization gap, robustness under compression/noise/adversarial attack.
81: 
82: ### 6. Fundamental Limitations & Why “Foolproof” Is Impossible
83: - **Arms race:** New generators are explicitly optimized to reduce detectable artifacts.
84: - **Generalization failure:** Detectors overfit to training generators/datasets; performance collapses on unseen architectures or real-world post-processing.
85: - **Adversarial attacks & post-editing:** Paraphrasing, re-encoding, mixing real + synthetic content, or targeted perturbations defeat most passive methods.
86: - **Access asymmetry:** Watermarking requires cooperation of the generator; passive methods do not.
87: - **Short / low-quality / mixed content:** Signals become statistically weak.
88: - **Human performance:** Meta-analyses show accuracy near chance without aids.
89: 
90: ### 7. Practical Recommendations
91: - Prefer **proactive** measures (standardized watermarking + content credentials) wherever generation can be controlled.
92: - Deploy **ensembles** rather than single detectors.
93: - Treat any single score as probabilistic evidence, not proof; combine with context, metadata, and human judgment.
94: - Maintain continuous evaluation pipelines against emerging models (open-source and commercial).
95: - For high-stakes use (journalism, law, elections, security): multi-modal analysis + provenance + expert review.
96: 
97: Key survey references for deeper reading include arXiv:2504.02898 (practical synthesis across modalities), arXiv:2402.00045 (multimedia survey), various 2024–2026 deepfake reviews covering image/video/audio, DetectGPT and watermarking papers (Kirchenbauer, SynthID), and audio surveys such as arXiv:2308.14970. Datasets and toolkits (e.g., DetectZoo) continue to expand for reproducible benchmarking.
98: 
99: In short: multi-layer detection (watermark + statistical + forensic + ensemble + human) raises the bar substantially, but absolute certainty remains unattainable against determined, state-of-the-art generation.

(End of file - total 99 lines)
</content>
