1: """
2: FINAL AI-Generated Text Detector
3: ===================================
4: Design philosophy: the old code's primary signal was a regex keyword list
5: ("delve", "leverage", "robust", "moreover"...) and an untrained character-
6: CNN. Two problems with that:
7:   1. Keyword lists go stale fast -- they target GPT-3.5-era stock phrasing
8:      and miss modern models, and they false-positive on humans who just
9:      happen to use those words (which, ironically, became common BECAUSE
10:      LLMs popularized them -- the signal degrades over time by design).
11:   2. An untrained CNN on character codes is random noise, same problem as
12:      the other detectors.
13: 
14: This version's primary signal is **perplexity + burstiness via a small
15: pretrained causal LM (GPT-2)**. This is the actual standard lightweight
16: approach (this is what the original DetectGPT / GPTZero-style tools are
17: built on): AI-generated text tends to sit in the model's high-probability
18: ("low perplexity") region everywhere, fairly uniformly. Human text has
19: more "burstiness" -- some very predictable spans, some very surprising
20: word choices, unevenly distributed.
21: 
22:   - GPT-2 (small, ~500MB, CPU-feasible) is used as a *probe* model, not
23:     because it's a strong LM, but because what we want is a *generic*
24:     next-token probability landscape to compare against text statistics --
25:     research on perplexity-based detection has shown even smaller probe
26:     models transfer reasonably across detecting larger generators.
27:   - Keyword/pattern matching is kept, but heavily downweighted and labeled
28:     explicitly as "stylistic indicators, weak signal, prone to false
29:     positives on non-native English writers" -- this is an important
30:     fairness note: penalizing "moreover/furthermore" usage disproportionately
31:     flags ESL writers and academic writing, not just AI text.
32: 
33: Setup:
34:   pip install transformers torch numpy --break-system-packages
35: First run downloads GPT-2 (~500MB) once, then cached offline.
36: """
